App Router: client-aborted requests permanently retain the whole request graph when the abort lands during a sequential DB-query chain (16.3.0, self-hosted)

### Summary

On our self-hosted App Router production app (Next 16.3.0, Node 22.23.2, Linux, `next start`), a request aborted by the client while a **sequential chain of database queries** is in flight permanently retains its entire request graph — `ServerResponse` (with `writableEnded: true, destroyed: true`), route `params`, promises, headers — surviving repeated forced full GCs and never draining. Under bot traffic (which aborts constantly) this compounds into OOM within hours; we mitigate today with a watchdog that restarts the container on an RSS threshold.

A **12-line synthetic page inside our app** reproduces it at 72–99%; the identical page in a fresh minimal app is clean, so one ambient enabler in a real app's setup is still unisolated (details below — we can run instrumented builds against our reproduction at will and will keep bisecting).

