Optimized conditional struct construction returns unclamped i64 field

A conditional constructing a struct with an i64 and f64 returns the input i64 instead of the upper bound under `-O`. The same source passes without optimization. This reproduces with the generic x86-64 target and with `-C target-cpu=x86-64-v3`.

Reproducer (`repro.rs`):
```rust
#[derive(Debug)]
struct Head { cell_y: i64, local_height: f64 }
fn main() {
    let level = Head { cell_y: std::hint::black_box(1i64 << 30), local_height: 0.0 };
    let head = if level.cell_y > (1 << 30) - 1 {
        Head { cell_y: (1 << 30) - 1, local_height: 1.0 }
    } else {
        Head { cell_y: level.cell_y.max(-(1 << 30)), local_height: level.local_height }
    };
    println!("{head:?}");
    assert_eq!(head.cell_y, (1 << 30) - 1);
    assert_eq!(head.local_height, 1.0);
}
```

Commands:
```
rustc repro.rs -o repro-debug && ./repro-debug
rustc -O repro.rs -o repro-release && ./repro-release
```

Expected both: `Head { cell_y: 1073741823, local_height: 1.0 }`.
Observed optimized: `Head { cell_y: 1073741824, local_height: 1.0 }`, followed by assertion failure. Debug prints the expected value.

Toolchain:
```
rustc 1.98.0 (88d9e12ae 2026-08-18)
binary: rustc
commit-hash: 88d9e12ae178fab0fb5cc050a94da85685d449ea
commit-date: 2026-08-18
host: x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu
release: 1.98.0
LLVM version: 22.1.8
```

Writing the integer field as `level.cell_y.clamp(-(1 << 30), (1 << 30) - 1)` and independently selecting the f64 field avoids the incorrect optimized result.


