In safe Rust, a branch `if c >= 0 && ... v[c as usize]` is compiled so that the `c < 0` test is dropped and `v[c as usize]` is bounds-checked with `c = -1`, panicking with `index out of bounds: the len is 4 but the index is 4294967295`. It reproduces at `opt-level=3` on `x86_64-pc-windows-msvc` (rustc 1.99.0 / LLVM 23.1.1; the original crate also with 1.98.0 / LLVM 22.1.8). `opt-level=2` and `-C llvm-args=-disable-cgp` give the correct result. `x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu` gives the correct result from the same IR (see below).

The cause is LLVM's CodeGenPrepare. `CodeGenPrepare::optimizeExt` moves `zext nneg i32 %c to i64` from the block guarded by `c >= 0` up into the load's block (`ExtFedByLoad->moveAfter(LI)`, to form an extending load), but it keeps the `nneg` flag. `optimizeExtUses` then rewrites the other uses of `%c`, including the guard `icmp slt i32 %c, 0`, as `trunc (zext nneg %c)`. When `c < 0` that value is poison, so the guard branches on poison and is undefined behavior. The x86 backend then folds the guard to false (see the assembly below). Before CodeGenPrepare the IR is correct.

