GVN assumes enum downcasts match the active variant when unifying enum aggregates

```rust
#![feature(custom_mir, core_intrinsics)]

use std::intrinsics::mir::*;

#[repr(u8)]
enum Foo {
    A(u8),
    B(u8),
}

#[custom_mir(dialect = "runtime")]
fn to_a(x: Foo) -> Foo {
    mir! {
        {
            RET = Foo::A(Field(Variant(x, 0), 0));
            Return()
        }
    }
}

fn main() {
    assert!(matches!(to_a(Foo::B(5)), Foo::A(5)));
}
```

This program should exit successfully, but when optimizations (notably GVN) are enabled, the assertion fails.

The problem is that we are accessing variant 0 of an enum whose active variant is 1, which is valid but sus. GVN assumes that means that the active variant must have been 0, and therefore that messy statement that loads the payload of variant 0 then constructs with variant 0 must be a no-op.

I suspect this bug can only be encountered by using custom_mir.

