Tracking Issue for `link_name_verbatim`

This is a tracking issue for `#![feature(link_name_verbatim)]`.

On Mach-O and x86 COFF, public symbol names are prefixed with `_` by default[^llvm-docs]. However in some cases it can be useful to avoid this, and use the exact symbol name that ends up in the final object file.

[^llvm-docs]: [LLVM docs](https://llvm.org/docs/LangRef.html#identifiers:~:text=m%3A%3Cmangling%3E)

Examples include:
1. `bindgen`, which has the actual symbol name from Clang, and where it's easier to set that directly instead of trying to figure out whether name mangling applies or not.
2. On macOS, Mach-O linkers support certain special (non-prefixed) symbols like `section$start$__DATA$__MY_SECTION` and `section$end$__DATA$__MY_SECTION`, whose address the linker will replace with respectively the start and end of the `__DATA,__MY_SECTION` section.

Example usage:

```rust
#![cfg(target_os = "macos")]
extern "C" {
    #[link_name(symbol = "_foo", modifiers = "+verbatim")]
    // Equivalent to `#[link_name = "foo"]`, as this would be mangled to `_foo`.
    fn bar();
}
```

This replaces the current implicit behaviour where a `\u{1}`-byte prefix is understood by LLVM (as documented [here](https://llvm.org/docs/LangRef.html#identifiers)) to mean "disable mangling of this symbol".
```rust
#![cfg(target_os = "macos")]
unsafe extern "C" {
    #[link_name = "\u{1}_foo"] // or "\x01_foo"
    fn bar();
}
```

This behaviour was not intentionally exposed by Rust, and is not supported by the Cranelift and GCC

