Thank you for filing a bug report! 🐛 Please provide a short summary of the bug,
along with any information you feel relevant to replicating the bug.
-->

I tried this code:

```rust
#![feature(async_drop)]
#![allow(incomplete_features)]
use std::{future::Future, pin::pin, task::{Context, Poll, Waker}};

struct Bar;
impl Drop for Bar {
    fn drop(&mut self) { println!("Bar dropped"); }
}

async fn outer() {
    let b = Bar;
    let _inner = async move { let _x = &b; };
    println!("end of outer");
}

fn main() {
    let mut f = pin!(outer());
    let mut cx = Context::from_waker(Waker::noop());
    while let Poll::Pending = f.as_mut().poll(&mut cx) {}
    println!("done");
}
```

```console
$ rustc --edition 2024 repro.rs && ./repro
end of outer
done
```


I expected:
```console
$ rustc --edition 2024 repro.rs && ./repro
end of outer
Bar dropped
done
```
This is what the same program prints without `#![feature(async_drop)]`. With the feature, `Bar` is never dropped, not even after `f` is dropped at the end of `main`. If `_inner` is a struct or tuple holding `b` instead of an async block, `Bar` is dropped.



